rary. A balanced presentation of multi-disciplinary approaches and multi-methodological approaches to Jewish studies, with a special emphasis on Jewish cultural studies and Jewish secularity.", "title": "Introduction to Jewish Cultures", "levelOfInstruction": "undergraduate", "prerequisitesText": "", "corequisitesText": "", "exclusionsText": "", "recommendedPreparation": "", "note": null, "division": "Arts and Science, Faculty of", "breadthRequirements": ["Creative and Cultural Representations (1)"], "distributionRequirements": ["Humanities"], "publicationSections": ["Centre for Jewish Studies"], "cmPublicationSections": [{"section": "Centre for Jewish Studies", "subSections": null}]}, "created": "2026-04-21@23:19:36.416", "modified": null, "lastSaved": 0, "primaryTeachMethod": "LEC", "faculty": {"code": "ARTSC", "name": "Faculty of Arts and Science"}, "coSec": {"code": "", "name": null}, "department": {"code": "JSP", "name": "Jewish Studies"}, "title": null, "maxCredit": 0.5, "minCredit": 0.5, "breadths": [{"org": {"code": "APSC", "name": "Faculty of Applied Science & Engineering"}, "breadthTypes": [{"kind": "ELECTIVE", "type": "Compl Studies", "description": "Complementary Studies", "code": "CS"}, {"kind": "ELECTIVE", "type": "Hum&SocSci", "description": "Humanities & Social Sciences", "code": "HSS"}]}, {"org": {"code": "ARTSC", "name": "Faculty of Arts and Science"}, "breadthTypes": [{"kind": "BREADTH", "type": "Creative Cultural", "description": "BR=1 Creative and Cultural Representation", "code": "BR=1"}]}], "notes": [{"name": "Course Note", "type": "COURSE", "content": ""}], "cancelInd": "N", "subscriptionTtb": false, "subscriptionOpenData": false, "tb1Active": false, "tb2Active": false, "fullyOnline": false, "primaryFull": false, "primaryWaitlistable": false}, {"id": "69dd3ea4830c3634bbb69968", "name": "Community", "ucName": null, "code": "CJS301H1", "sectionCode": "S", "campus": "St. George", "sessions": ["20271"], "sections": [{"name": "LEC0101", "type": "Lecture", "teachMethod": "LEC", "sectionNumber": "0101", "meetingTimes": null, "firstMeeting": null, "instructors": null, "currentEnrolment": null, "maxEnrolment": null, "subTitle": null, "cancelInd": null, "waitlistInd": null, "deliveryModes": [], "currentWaitlist": null, "enrolmentInd": null, "tbaInd": null, "openLimitInd": null, "notes": [{"name": "Section Note", "type": "SECTION", "content": ""}], "enrolmentControls": null, "linkedMeetingSections": null}], "duration": null, "cmCourseInfo": {"description": "Ethno-religious groups like Jewry or the Jews are not merely aggregates of individuals but rather collectives whose members are bound to each other in a common historical identity, life, and fate. To understand any such group, we must therefore understand what binds these people together into a community, why the burdens and benefits or responsibilities and resources of the community are distributed among its members as they are, and both how and how well internal conflicts are managed. This course considers the ways in which the Jewish community -- in its local and global guises -- has reflected upon and dealt with these issues in classical and contemporary contexts.", "title": "Community: Jewish Principles and Policies", "levelOfInstruction": "undergraduate", "prerequisitesText": "", "corequisitesText": "", "exclusionsText": "", "recommendedPreparation": "", "note": null, "division": "Arts and Science, Faculty of", "breadthRequirements": ["Thought, Belief and Behaviour (2)"], "distributionRequirements": ["Humanities"], "publicationSections": ["Centre for Jewish Studies"], "cmPublicationSections": [{"section": "Centre for Jewish Studies", "subSections": null}]}, "created": "2026-04-21@23:19:36.416", "modified": null, "lastSaved": 0, "primaryTeachMethod": "LEC", "faculty": {"code": "ARTSC", "name": "Faculty of Arts and Science"}, "coSec": {"code": "", "name": null}, "department": {"code": "JSP", "name": "Jewish Studies"}, "title": null, "maxCredit": 0.5, "minCredit": 0.5, "breadths": [{"org": {"code": "ARTSC", "name": "Faculty of Arts and Science"}, "breadthTypes": [{"kind": "BREADTH", "type": "Thought", "description": "BR=2 Thought, Belief and Behaviour", "code": "BR=2"}]}], "notes": [{"name": "Course Note", "type": "COURSE", "content": ""}], "cancelInd": "N", "subscriptionTtb": false, "subscriptionOpenData": false, "tb1Active": false, "tb2Active": false, "fullyOnline": false, "primaryFull": false, "primaryWaitlistable": false}, {"id": "69dd3ea4830c3634bbb69969", "name": "Who's a Jew? Theory, Myth, and Practice", "ucName": null, "code": "CJS330H1", "sectionCode": "F", "campus": "St. George", "sessions": ["20269"], "sections": [{"name": "LEC0101", "type": "Lecture", "teachMethod": "LEC", "sectionNumber": "0101", "meetingTimes": null, "firstMeeting": null, "instructors": null, "currentEnrolment": null, "maxEnrolment": null, "subTitle": null, "cancelInd": null, "waitlistInd": null, "deliveryModes": [], "currentWaitlist": null, "enrolmentInd": null, "tbaInd": null, "openLimitInd": null, "notes": [{"name": "Section Note", "type": "SECTION", "content": ""}], "enrolmentControls": null, "linkedMeetingSections": null}], "duration": null, "cmCourseInfo": {"description": "This course introduces students to the host of core concepts in terms of which Jewish identity has been and continues to be defined and debated. Topics include: the difference between insiders and outsiders; collective vs individual identity; the nature of the bond between group members; identification across time, space, and disagreements; social and gendered hierarchies; joining and leaving the group; the identities of outsiders.", "title": "Who's a Jew? Theory, Myth, and Practice", "levelOfInstruction": "undergraduate", "prerequisitesText": null, "corequisitesText": null, "exclusionsText": "CJS290H1 (Topics in Jewish Studies: Who is a Jew?), offered in Fall 2014 and Fall 2015", "recommendedPreparation": "CJS200H1 or CJS201H1", "note": null, "division": "Arts and Science, Faculty of", "breadthRequirements": ["Thought, Belief and Behaviour (2)"], "distributionRequirements": ["Humanities"], "publicationSections": ["Centre for Jewish Studies"], "cmPublicationSections": [{"section": "Centre for Jewish Studies", "subSections": null}]}, "created": "2026-04-21@23:19:36.416", "modified": null, "lastSaved": 0, "primaryTeachMethod": "LEC", "faculty": {"code": "ARTSC", "name": "Faculty of Arts and Science"}, "coSec": {"code": "", "name": null}, "department": {"code": "JSP", "name": "Jewish Studies"}, "title": null, "maxCredit": 0.5, "minCredit": 0.5, "breadths": [{"org": {"code": "APSC", "name": "Faculty of Applied Science & Engineering"}, "breadthTypes": [{"kind": "ELECTIVE", "type": "Compl Studies", "description": "Complementary Studies", "code": "CS"}, {"kind": "ELECTIVE", "type": "Hum&SocSci", "description": "Humanities & Social Sciences", "code": "HSS"}]}, {"org": {"code": "ARTSC", "name": "Faculty of Arts and Science"}, "breadthTypes": [{"kind": "BREADTH", "type": "Thought", "description": "BR=2 Thought, Belief and Behaviour", "code": "BR=2"}]}], "notes": [{"name": "Course Note", "type": "COURSE", "content": ""}], "cancelInd": "", "subscriptionTtb": false, "subscriptionOpenData": false, "tb1Active": false, "tb2Active": false, "fullyOnline": false, "primaryFull": false, "primaryWaitlistable": false}, {"id": "69dd3ea4830c3634bbb6996a", "name": "Zionism, Anti-Zionism, and Post-Zionism", "ucName": null, "code": "CJS370H1", "sectionCode": "S", "campus": "St. George", "sessions": ["20271"], "sections": [{"name": "LEC0101", "type": "Lecture", "teachMethod": "LEC", "sectionNumber": "0101", "meetingTimes": null, "firstMeeting": null, "instructors": null, "currentEnrolment": null, "maxEnrolment": null, "subTitle": null, "cancelInd": null, "waitlistInd": null, "deliveryModes": [], "currentWaitlist": null, "enrolmentInd": null, "tbaInd": null, "openLimitInd": null, "notes": [{"name": "Section Note", "type": "SECTION", "content": ""}], "enrolmentControls": null, "linkedMeetingSections": null}], "duration": null, "cmCourseInfo": {"description": "Zionism proposed changing not only Jews\u2019 socio-political condition, but also their basic self-conception. This proposal sparked debates inside and outside the movement. Internally, Zionists of various sorts argued about priorities and practices, while, externally, ultraorthodox, Reform, and assimilationist Jews rejected Zionism on different grounds. Even after Israel\u2019s establishment, Jews within and beyond the new state\u2019s borders continued to debate Zionism\u2019s purpose and primacy in Jewish life. These intense debates persist, as Jewish communities now splinter into Zionist, anti-Zionist, and post-Zionist camps and their competing assessments of Jewish history over the past 150 years. What reasons do Jews today identify as Zionist, anti-Zionist, or post-Zionist? How should Jews relate to Jewish identities that contradict their own? How should Jews\u2019 weigh criticisms of Zionism from outside their community in assessing Zionism\u2019s realities and possibilities? This course addresses these questions to understand better the live debates about Jews\u2019 identity, diversity, flourishing, and future.", "title": "Zionism, Anti-Zionism, and Post-Zionism", "levelOfInstruction": null, "prerequisitesText": "<p>6.0 credits</p>", "corequisitesText": "", "exclusionsText": "<p>CJS290H1 (Topic: <i>Zionism, Anti-Zionism, Post-Zionism</i>) offered in Winter 2025 and Winter 2026.</p>", "recommendedPreparation": "<p>CJS200H1/ CJS201H1</p>", "note": null, "division": "Arts and Science, Faculty of", "breadthRequirements": ["Thought, Belief and Behaviour (2)"], "distributionRequirements": null, "publicationSections": ["Centre for Jewish Studies"], "cmPublicationSections": [{"section": "Centre for Jewish Studies", "subSections": null}]}, "created": "2026-04-21@23:19:36.416", "modified": null, "lastSaved": 0, "primaryTeachMethod": "LEC", "faculty": {"code": "AR